We have some strange behavior in our audio application using the ALC 888=20
hardware in ubuntu 10.10 with the hda-intel driver . Basically the main=20
problem seems to be a buffer or memory management issue. Once a playback=20
is stopped the very last sample is repeated forever, independently from=20
sampling rate or sound file format. This sample leads to a constant DC=20
voltage after the DA converters.

We already tried the following steps:

1.) we changed a line in hda_intel.c to reset the fifo

--- a/sound/pci/hda/hda_intel.c
+++ b/sound/pci/hda/hda_intel.c
@@ -1797,7 +1797,7 @@ static int azx_pcm_hw_free(struct snd_pcm_substream=
 *substream)
  	/* reset BDL address */
  	azx_sd_writel(azx_dev, SD_BDLPL, 0);
  	azx_sd_writel(azx_dev, SD_BDLPU, 0);
-	azx_sd_writel(azx_dev, SD_CTL, 0);
+	azx_stream_reset(chip, azx_dev);
  	azx_dev->bufsize =3D 0;
  	azx_dev->period_bytes =3D 0;
  	azx_dev->format_val =3D 0;


2.) we set 2 parameters in the .config file before kernel compiling

CONFIG_SND_HDA_POWER_SAVE=3Dy
CONFIG_SND_HDA_POWER_SAVE_DEFAULT=3D0

Both solutions didn=C2=B4t work for us - still same problem. Has anyone=20
another idea how to solve this?

Would be great if we could fix this.

Frank


